<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-probability-in-real-life" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Probability in Real Life</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Appreciate the application of probability in day-to-day life</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>10 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>Learners recognise that probability is embedded in real decisions around them and connect the tools of this chapter to familiar contexts.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Pose the following to pairs: <em>"Think of three situations in Kenyan daily life where someone makes a decision under uncertainty. What information would help them? Could you put a number on the chance?"</em> Pairs discuss then share with the class. Group responses on the board under: health, agriculture, transport, sport, business and finance. Follow up: <em>"Which tools we have learned this term could help in each case?"</em>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity>
        <title>Mobile Phones for the School</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A school in Nairobi buys 3 mobile phones for its computer lab. The manufacturer states that each phone has a 90% chance of lasting 2 years without a major fault. Assume the phones are independent.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>What is the probability that all three phones last 2 years without a fault?</p></li>
                    <li><p>What is the probability that at least one phone develops a fault within 2 years?</p></li>
                    <li><p>The school has a budget to replace exactly one phone if needed. What is the probability that more than one phone faults, leaving the school unable to cover the cost?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="lesson-plan">
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Each phone works with probability 0.9. Since the phones are independent:
                            <md>P(\text{all 3 work}) = 0.9 \times 0.9 \times 0.9 = 0.9^3 = 0.729.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use the complement:
                            <md>P(\text{at least one fault}) = 1 - 0.729 = 0.271.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            More than one fault means exactly 2 or exactly 3 faults.
                            <md>P(\text{exactly 2 faults}) = \binom{3}{2}(0.1)^2(0.9) = 3 \times 0.01 \times 0.9 = 0.027.</md>
                            <md>P(\text{exactly 3 faults}) = (0.1)^3 = 0.001.</md>
                            <md>P(\text{more than 1 fault}) = 0.027 + 0.001 = 0.028.</md>
                            Only about a 3% risk — the one-replacement budget is a reasonable plan.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>Learners see how the probability tools from this chapter arise naturally in real-world decision-making.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Connect the activity back to the Discovery discussion. The school scenario used the multiplication rule (independent events) and the complement. Draw out from learners how each tool they have learned maps to a real application:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Multiplication rule and tree diagrams <m>\rightarrow</m> multi-stage events such as diagnostic tests and weather forecasts.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Complement <m>\rightarrow</m> <q>at least one</q> problems in quality control and genetics.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Total probability (summing paths in a tree) <m>\rightarrow</m> combining two possible causes, such as good rain versus poor rain affecting a harvest.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                    Emphasise two key limitations: probability gives the <em>chance</em>, not a guarantee; and the law of large numbers means probability predictions become reliable only over many repeated trials.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Probability is a tool for decision-making under uncertainty. A high probability does not mean certainty, and a low probability does not mean impossible.</p></li>
                <li><p>In agriculture, medicine, and insurance, probability helps us plan for outcomes we cannot control.</p></li>
                <li><p><term>Law of large numbers:</term> over many repeated trials, the experimental relative frequency gets closer and closer to the theoretical probability.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>A Malaria Diagnostic Test</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A rapid malaria test is used at a clinic in Kisumu. The test correctly identifies malaria in 95% of cases where malaria is present. It correctly gives a negative result in 90% of cases where malaria is absent. In this area, 20% of patients presenting with fever have malaria.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Draw a tree diagram and find the probability that a randomly selected fever patient tests positive.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Out of 1000 fever patients, estimate how many will test positive but do not actually have malaria.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <!-- TODO: TikZ tree diagram — fig-tree-malaria
                 2-level tree: Malaria(0.2)/No Malaria(0.8) → Positive/Negative
                 From Malaria: Positive(0.95), Negative(0.05)
                 From No Malaria: Positive(0.10), Negative(0.90)
                 Terminal probs: 0.190, 0.010, 0.080, 0.720 -->
            <p>
                The tree has two stages: whether the patient has malaria, then whether the test result is positive. The four terminal path probabilities are:
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Terminal path probabilities for the malaria test</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Path</cell>
                        <cell>Calculation</cell>
                        <cell>Probability</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Malaria, Positive</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.20 \times 0.95</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.190</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Malaria, Negative</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.20 \times 0.05</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.010</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>No Malaria, Positive</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.80 \times 0.10</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.080</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>No Malaria, Negative</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.80 \times 0.90</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.720</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>1.000 ✓</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A patient tests positive via two paths: Malaria+Positive or No Malaria+Positive.
                            <md>P(\text{positive}) = 0.190 + 0.080 = 0.270.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A false positive occurs on the path No Malaria, Positive. Out of 1000 patients:
                            <md>0.080 \times 1000 = 80 \text{ false positives}.</md>
                            Of the <m>0.270 \times 1000 = 270</m> patients who test positive, 80 (about 30%) do not actually have malaria. This is why clinical judgement alongside the test is essential.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Quality Control in a Light Bulb Factory</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A factory in Eldoret produces energy-saving light bulbs. 4% of the bulbs produced are defective. A hardware shop orders a box of 5 bulbs.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>What is the probability that all 5 bulbs work correctly?</p></li>
                    <li><p>What is the probability that at least one bulb is defective?</p></li>
                    <li><p>The factory offers a full refund if any bulb in the box is defective. Approximately what fraction of boxes sold will require a refund?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Each bulb is defective with probability 0.04, independently.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(\text{all 5 work}) = (0.96)^5 \approx 0.815.</md>
                            About 81.5% of boxes contain all working bulbs.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(\text{at least one defective}) = 1 - 0.815 = 0.185.</md>
                            About 18.5% of boxes contain at least one defective bulb.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            About 18.5% of boxes — roughly 1 in 5 — will require a refund. This information helps the factory price the guarantee appropriately.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Weather and Farming in Nakuru</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A farmer in Nakuru plants wheat. Historical records show that P(good rains) = 0.55. If the rains are good, P(profitable wheat harvest) = 0.80. If the rains are poor, P(profitable wheat harvest) = 0.20.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the probability that the wheat harvest is profitable this year.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The farmer also plants maize on a second plot during the same season. For maize: P(profitable | good rains) = 0.70, and P(profitable | poor rains) = 0.45. Since both crops share the same rain season, the weather outcome is the same for both plots. Find the probability that both crops are profitable.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using a tree diagram with two first-level branches (good rain, poor rain):
                            <md>P(\text{profitable wheat}) = 0.55 \times 0.80 + 0.45 \times 0.20 = 0.440 + 0.090 = 0.530.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Because the rain outcome is shared, we consider the two cases separately:
                            <md>P(\text{both} \mid \text{good rain}) = 0.80 \times 0.70 = 0.560.</md>
                            <md>P(\text{both} \mid \text{poor rain}) = 0.20 \times 0.45 = 0.090.</md>
                            <md>P(\text{both profitable}) = 0.55 \times 0.560 + 0.45 \times 0.090 = 0.308 + 0.041 = 0.349.</md>
                            About 34.9% of years will see both crops profitable.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Note: we cannot find this answer simply by multiplying P(wheat profitable) by P(maize profitable). Both crops depend on the same rainfall, so they are not independent.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Both parents carry one recessive sickle cell gene. Each of their children, independently, has a probability of <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> of inheriting the condition. The couple has 3 children.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find the probability that none of the 3 children has sickle cell.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that at least one of the 3 children has sickle cell.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>\left(\dfrac{3}{4}\right)^3 = \dfrac{27}{64}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>1 - \dfrac{27}{64} = \dfrac{37}{64}</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The probability that a matatu from Thika to Nairobi leaves on time is 0.7. If it leaves on time, the probability it arrives on time is 0.9. If it does not leave on time, the probability it arrives on time is 0.3.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find the probability that the matatu arrives on time.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Out of 1000 journeys, estimate how many will arrive on time and also left on time.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>0.7 \times 0.9 + 0.3 \times 0.3 = 0.63 + 0.09 = 0.72</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0.63 \times 1000 = 630</m> journeys</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An insurance company charges KSh 600 per month per policy. Based on records, 10% of policyholders make a claim each month, with an average claim of KSh 4,000.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>What is the expected payout per policy per month?</p></li>
                        <li><p>What is the average profit per policy per month?</p></li>
                        <li><p>If the company has 500 policyholders, what is its expected total monthly profit?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>KSh <m>0.10 \times 4000 = 400</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>KSh <m>600 - 400 = 200</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>KSh <m>500 \times 200 = 100{,}000</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Kenya's under-20 football team plays 3 matches in a group stage. The probability of winning any single match is 0.55, independently. The team qualifies for the knockout round by winning at least 2 of the 3 matches.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find the probability that Kenya wins exactly 2 of the 3 matches.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that Kenya wins all 3 matches.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that Kenya qualifies.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>\binom{3}{2}(0.55)^2(0.45) = 3 \times 0.3025 \times 0.45 \approx 0.408</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>(0.55)^3 \approx 0.166</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0.408 + 0.166 \approx 0.574</m> (about 57%)</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

